course: world
lesson: 93
title: Lungs, air and keeping fit
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The air you breathe in is about one fifth oxygen; most of the rest is nitrogen, which your body does not use. The air you breathe out has less oxygen and more carbon dioxide.
explain: To breathe in, the diaphragm under your lungs tightens and moves down and your ribs move up and out; the chest gets bigger and air rushes in. When they relax, air is pushed out.
explain: Your nose and windpipe clean the air: hairs and sticky mucus trap dust and germs, and tiny moving hairs sweep the mucus up so you swallow or cough it away.
explain: When you run, your muscles need much more oxygen, so you breathe faster and deeper and your heart beats faster to carry it. Regular exercise makes the heart stronger, so it pumps more blood with each beat.
explain: Example: a fit person's pulse may be slower at rest and goes back to normal quickly after a race — so how fast you recover is a good test of fitness.
fact: Air is about one fifth oxygen and nearly four fifths nitrogen.
fact: Breathed-out air contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide than breathed-in air.
fact: When the diaphragm tightens and moves down, the chest gets bigger and air flows into the lungs.
fact: Hairs and sticky mucus in the nose and airways trap dust and germs.
fact: During exercise we breathe faster and deeper to get more oxygen to the muscles.
fact: During exercise the heart beats faster to carry more oxygen to the muscles.
fact: Regular exercise makes the heart stronger, so it pumps more blood with each beat.
fact: A fit person's heart rate returns to normal quickly after exercise.
fact: In asthma the airways become narrow, which makes breathing hard; an inhaler helps to open them.
fact: High up on mountains the air is thinner, so each breath brings in less oxygen.
fact: After some weeks high on a mountain, the body slowly gets used to working with less oxygen.
fact: Smoke and polluted air damage the lungs.
exercise: About how much of the air is oxygen? | About one fifth | About half | Almost all of it | About one hundredth
exercise: Which gas makes up most of the air? | Nitrogen | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Water vapour
exercise: How is breathed-out air different from breathed-in air? | It has less oxygen and more carbon dioxide | It has no oxygen at all | It has more oxygen and less carbon dioxide | It is exactly the same air
exercise: What happens when the diaphragm tightens and moves down? | The chest gets bigger and air flows in | The chest gets smaller and air is pushed out | The heart stops beating for a moment | Food moves down to the stomach
exercise: What traps dust and germs in your nose? | Hairs and sticky mucus | The tongue and the lips | The front teeth and gums | The diaphragm and the ribs
exercise: Why do you breathe faster when you run? | Your muscles need more oxygen | Your lungs get smaller when you run | Running fills the lungs with dust | Your body needs to lose oxygen
exercise: Why does your heart beat faster during exercise? | To carry more oxygen to the muscles | To make you feel scared and jumpy inside | To cool the blood down before it gets hot | To stop the lungs from working too hard
exercise: What does regular exercise do to the heart? | Makes it stronger so it pumps more with each beat | Makes it smaller and weaker | Makes it beat fast all the time | Has no effect on the heart
exercise: What is a good sign that someone is fit? | Their pulse returns to normal quickly after exercise | Their pulse stays high for an hour | They never breathe faster when running | They sweat even when resting
exercise: What happens to the airways during an asthma attack? | They become narrow | They become wider | They fill with blood | They stop making mucus
exercise: What does an inhaler for asthma do? | Helps to open the narrowed airways | Fills the lungs with pure nitrogen | Makes the heart beat slower | Cleans dust out of the nose
exercise: Why is it harder to run high up on a mountain? | Each breath brings in less oxygen | There is no gravity at all up there | The air is far too heavy to breathe in | Mountains are always much too hot to run on
exercise: Why should you breathe through your nose in dusty air? | The nose traps dust before it reaches the lungs | The nose makes the air dustier | The nose takes in more nitrogen than the mouth does | The mouth cannot take in air
test: Two children run the same race. One's pulse is back to normal in 2 minutes, the other's in 10 minutes. Which is probably fitter? | The one who recovers in 2 minutes | The one who recovers in 10 minutes | They are exactly as fit | Pulse tells nothing about fitness
test: Which is true? | We breathe out some oxygen as well as carbon dioxide | We breathe out only carbon dioxide | The air is mostly oxygen | Nitrogen is what our cells need most
test: Put your hands on your ribs and breathe in deeply. What do you feel? | The ribs move up and out | The ribs move in and down | Nothing moves at all | The ribs move only when you breathe out
test: A footballer trains high in the mountains for weeks. Why might this help? | Her body gets used to working with less oxygen | The mountain air has extra oxygen | Mountains have weaker gravity, so running is much easier | Cold air makes her lungs bigger
test: After a day in a smoky city, Ana coughs up mucus with dark specks in it. What were the specks? | Dust and smoke specks trapped by the airways | Old red blood cells that came up from the heart | Bits of the lungs themselves | Oxygen that has turned dark
test: Why does a person with asthma find it hard to blow out candles? | Narrow airways let less air in and out | Their lungs make far too much oxygen to blow | Their diaphragm is much too strong to control | Candles need lots of carbon dioxide to burn
test: If someone never exercises, what usually happens to their heart and muscles? | They become weaker | They become stronger anyway | They grow bigger with rest | They stop using oxygen
test: Why are you still breathing hard for a short while after you stop running? | Your body is still catching up on the oxygen it needs | Your lungs are full of nitrogen | Your heart has stopped for a moment and needs to restart | Running has made your lungs smaller
test: Why is smoking especially bad for a runner? | It damages the lungs so less oxygen reaches the muscles | It makes the legs too heavy | It gives the lungs too much oxygen | It stops the heart from beating faster
test: why am I puffing so hard after running | You breathe faster and deeper to get more oxygen to your muscles | Your lungs get smaller when you run | You need to breathe out all the oxygen | Running makes the air thinner
test: why's it harder to breathe up a big mountain | The air is thinner, so each breath brings less oxygen | The air up there has no oxygen at all, only nitrogen gas | The cold freezes your lungs | The mountain pulls the air downhill
test: Ava says we breathe out only carbon dioxide and no oxygen. true? | No, breathed-out air has less oxygen and more carbon dioxide | Yes, all the oxygen stays in the body | Yes, breathed-out air is pure carbon dioxide | No, breathed-out air has more oxygen than air in
test: my mate reckons air is mostly oxygen. right? | No, air is nearly four fifths nitrogen and one fifth oxygen | Yes, air is mostly oxygen | Yes, air is about half oxygen | No, air is mostly carbon dioxide
exam: Ascertain the proportion of oxygen in the air. | About one fifth | About four fifths | About one half | About one tenth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises breathed-out air in comparison with breathed-in air? | It contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide | It contains more oxygen and less carbon dioxide | It contains no oxygen whatsoever | It is identical in composition
exam: Identify the action by which air is drawn into the lungs. | The diaphragm tightens and moves down, enlarging the chest | The diaphragm relaxes and moves up, shrinking the chest | The heart pumps air into the lungs | The stomach pushes air upwards into the lungs
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning breathing high upon mountains. | Each breath brings in less oxygen, the air being thinner | Each breath brings in more oxygen, the air being cleaner | Breathing is identical at every height | The air contains no oxygen above the clouds
